    "result_text": "The full test suite (the acceptance test) passes with `BUILD SUCCESS`. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI added a `timezone` field to the owner API, derived from the owner's locality (region) via the fixed region-to-timezone table.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`TimezoneResolver.java`** (new util) \u2014 a single-responsibility class holding the fixed region\u2192IANA-timezone table (`NSW\u2192Australia/Sydney`, `VIC\u2192Australia/Melbourne`, `QLD\u2192Australia/Brisbane`), with `forRegion(region)` returning the IANA name or `null` when the region maps to nothing. This mirrors the existing `LocalityResolver` style and keeps the timezone table out of unrelated classes.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a `timezone` mapping that reuses the existing `LocalityResolver.fromCustomerCode(...)` locality computation and feeds it to `TimezoneResolver.forRegion(...)`, so the timezone is always consistent with the reported `locality` (no duplicated region logic).\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 declared the read-only `timezone` string field on the owner schema (right after `locality`), documenting the region-to-timezone table, so the generated `OwnerDto` gets the field.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreReturnsTimezoneForRegion` now confirms an owner in Sydney (postcode 2000 \u2192 NSW) returns `locality: \"NSW\"` and `timezone: \"Australia/Sydney\"`.",
